{"id":"23fd7456-a4b5-430f-887c-2a155276f739","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885987,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    if 'threshold' not in case or 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields 'threshold' or 'calls'\\\")\\n\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"'calls' must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n\\n    for call in calls:\\n        # Validate each call has required fields\\n        required_fields = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\n        for field in required_fields:\\n            if field not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Call missing required field '{field}'\\\")\\n\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        pred_tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n\\n        # Ensure prediction_tokens is at least 1 (as per \\\"at least one prediction token\\\")\\n        if pred_tokens < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"prediction_tokens must be >= 1, got {pred_tokens}\\\")\\n\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\\n\\n        # Round to avoid floating point artifacts (8 decimal places)\\n        normalized_gain = round(normalized_gain, 8)\\n\\n        accepted = bool(parseable) and normalized_gain >= threshold\\n\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            'accepted': accepted,\\n            'call_id': call_id,\\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\\n        })\\n\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n\\n    return {\\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_call_ids,\\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task AGENT-TF-01 based on the description. The case payload contains threshold (number) and calls (list of objects each with call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable). We need to compute normalized gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens? Wait, the description: \"For each call subtract loss with the call from loss without it. Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" So gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call. Then normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens? Or maybe \"by at least one prediction token\" means we divide by prediction_tokens, but ensure denominator at least 1? But prediction_tokens is integer, could be 0? Not specified but likely positive. However \"at least one prediction token\" suggests we use max(prediction_tokens, 1) if prediction_tokens can be 0. But in fixtures, all are >=2. We'll use prediction_tokens directly. Then condition: parseable must be true and normalized_gain >= threshold. Then accepted call_ids are those that meet condition. Diagnostics list for each call in original order with fields: accepted (boolean), call_id (string), normalized_gain (number). normalized_gain should be rounded? The description: \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" How rounded? Probably round to some precision. Not specified. In the schema, normalized_gain is number (float). We can round to e.g., 8 decimal places? But maybe just keep as float without rounding? However they say \"Return ... rounded per-call diagnostics.\" Could be that we round each normalized_gain to some number of decimals? In the output schema it's just number. The example fixture outputs? Not given. We'll compute normalized_gain as float with full precision, but maybe round to something like 8 decimals? The problem statement doesn't specify rounding precision. Perhaps they want rounding to e.g., 8 decimal places because the input numbers have many decimals. Or maybe they want to round to the same precision as input? The input numbers are up to 8 decimal places. I'd compute and not round, but the phrase \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" might imply that we round each normalized_gain to a reasonable number of decimals, maybe 8. But to be safe, I'll compute as float and not round, because the schema says number, and returning a float is fine. However, the output expected may require rounding to avoid floating point artifacts? The test likely checks approximate equality. I'll compute and not round, but we can do round(value, 8) to be safe. The problem says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\", so I think rounding is expected. I'll round normalized_gain to 8 decimal places.\n\nAlso \"Require both parseability and normalized gain at or above threshold.\" So parseable must be true, normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\n\"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\" So iterate over calls list in order.\n\n\"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So output dict with accepted_call_ids (list of strings in order of acceptance? The order? It says \"accepted_call_ids\" presumably in the order they appear in calls. So we can collect accepted call_ids in original order.\n\n\"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we don't need any external data.\n\n\"Validate every required field before computing.\" So check that each call has required fields: call_id, loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. Also threshold present. If missing, raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) that takes a dict (the payload). Extract threshold and calls. For each call, compute gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call, normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens (float). Then accepted = parseable and normalized_gain >= threshold. Append to diagnostics dict with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain (rounded). Append call_id to accepted list if accepted. Return dict with accepted_call_ids and diagnostics.\n\nWe must ensure not to mutate case. We'll make copies as needed.\n\nNow, the output contract: The solve(case) must return a JSON-serializable object that matches the output structural schema. Our function returns a dict with keys accepted_call_ids and diagnostics. That matches.\n\nWe need to output the Python source as a JSON string within {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with that key.\n\nWe must escape the Python code properly: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\".\n\nI'll write the Python code.\n\nBe careful: The case argument is the payload inner object, not the whole fixture row. So we don't have case_id etc. The code should assume case is a dict with threshold and calls.\n\nAlso note: \"dynamicKeysFrom means the object keys are arbitrary JSON strings that must be copied verbatim from the indicated input location; never parse, coerce, numerically validate, or impose a naming pattern on those keys.\" This is in schema description but not relevant to our output, as our output doesn't have dynamic keys.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2164,"completion_tokens":1555,"total_tokens":3719,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1082},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":116},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}